        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "5d1cacab-c9b0-33de-b050-741feffa03f9",
                "word": "scientisme",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "538bddf3-7dae-422b-8465-8424810e369f",
                        "entry_id": "5d1cacab-c9b0-33de-b050-741feffa03f9",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "scientismes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "2d5e0c42-a338-38a5-b47e-fdf03abdd37c",
                        "entry_id": "5d1cacab-c9b0-33de-b050-741feffa03f9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Doctrine selon laquelle la connaissance scientifique permettrait d'échapper à l'ignorance et l'obscurantisme, foi dans l'application des principes de la science dans tous les domaines."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d5593650-b816-4e29-ab63-effbf9bb56eb",
                                "sense_id": "2d5e0c42-a338-38a5-b47e-fdf03abdd37c",
                                "text": "Quelle plus belle preuve fourniraient les partisans de ce scientisme effréné que de réussir effectivement, et plus simplement en laboratoire, à influencer les actions humaines dans le domaine de la vie sociale et politique ?"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "6c4c226f-67b9-3185-b4d2-819dd3e76dea",
                        "entry_id": "5d1cacab-c9b0-33de-b050-741feffa03f9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Paradigme de la recherche scientifique qui s'appuie exclusivement sur la vraisemblance d'un jeu de données selon les prédictions d'un seul modèle théorique de référence afin de valider une nouvelle découverte. Elle s'inscrit dans le cadre de l'inférence statistique fréquentiste s'opposant à l’inférence bayésienne."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1c0de408-bef6-3b56-aa62-cdb4f379033c",
                        "entry_id": "5d1cacab-c9b0-33de-b050-741feffa03f9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Terme péjoratif faisant référence à une vision du monde dans laquelle on donne plus d'importance à un modèle fondé sur la base d'anciens résultats expérimentaux, plutôt qu'à des résultats d'expériences récents susceptibles d'invalider la pertinence prédictive du modèle impliqué."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "c7deccf8-6be4-4b9a-9a0f-25870169175b",
                        "source_entry_id": "5d1cacab-c9b0-33de-b050-741feffa03f9",
                        "target_lang_word": "scientism",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "scientism",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9dfa87b4-068c-3e75-90c0-ec457e2d9e4f",
                                        "entry_id": "9b386434-cf3f-341a-9747-a3c8396f1d1e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The belief that the scientific method and the assumptions and research methods of the physical sciences are applicable to all other disciplines (such as the humanities and social sciences), or that those other disciplines are not as valuable."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "75ec4637-f880-4e53-9056-d4003c51fdd9",
                                                "sense_id": "9dfa87b4-068c-3e75-90c0-ec457e2d9e4f",
                                                "text": "Finally, with good reason, this psychology aims to be a psychology of a purely empiriological type. It is pervaded and overwhelmed on every side, however, by a pseudometaphysics of the most vulgar character, which Freud is all the less anxious to dispense with as he imagines he has no philosophy or metaphysics at all. I say pseudometaphysics of the most vulgar type, because it combines all the prejudices of deterministic, mechanistic scientism with all the prejudices of irrationalism.",
                                                "translation": "Freudisme et psychanalyse",
                                                "english": "Freudisme et psychanalyse"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7a814e0d-7522-41c0-a8e8-de842dd5bbfc",
                                                "sense_id": "9dfa87b4-068c-3e75-90c0-ec457e2d9e4f",
                                                "text": "This claim, espoused by scientific naturalists, is called scientism, the view that science is the paradigm of truth and rationality. There are two forms of scientism: strong and weak. Strong scientism […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e21c20c2-0b48-4bfd-9af7-35653250268c",
                                                "sense_id": "9dfa87b4-068c-3e75-90c0-ec457e2d9e4f",
                                                "text": "This is the view of scientism. The historical roots of scientism can probably be traced to the Enlightenment with its ideology of progress and perfectibility. Perhaps its best-known historical advocate is the French social philosopher Auguste […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "62d73ac4-74fe-41fc-95a8-eb702b0216dd",
                                                "sense_id": "9dfa87b4-068c-3e75-90c0-ec457e2d9e4f",
                                                "text": "Habermas's work of the middle and late 1960s is centrally concerned with the philosophical and sociological issues raised by scientism, the reduction of all knowledge to that furnished by the empirical sciences, where these are conceived as […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7a4cd5ed-5bd1-41f6-b94d-0d1d57f5b4c8",
                                                "sense_id": "9dfa87b4-068c-3e75-90c0-ec457e2d9e4f",
                                                "text": "'Scientism means ... that we no longer understand science as one form of possible knowledge, but rather identify knowledge with science.' Habermas's critique of scientism focuses on its relation to positivism, since positivism provides […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a186a5f6-1e9c-49f0-9b38-d2c839b8e594",
                                                "sense_id": "9dfa87b4-068c-3e75-90c0-ec457e2d9e4f",
                                                "text": "First, I want to make clear that what I am attacking is not science but scientism: the mistaken belief that the natural sciences (physics, chemistry, biology and their derivatives) can or will give a complete description and even explanation of […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "00a09131-7e1b-3ba6-8031-28536a32f56f",
                                        "entry_id": "9b386434-cf3f-341a-9747-a3c8396f1d1e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The belief that all truth is exclusively discovered through science."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
